<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Robert Muldoon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1921">1921</a>–<a href="/years/1992">1992</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Sir Robert Muldoon</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Robert Muldoon was Prime Minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> from 1975 to 1984 and also his own finance minister. Leading the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-party-south-africa">National Party</a>, he responded to oil shocks and loss of British markets with heavy state intervention, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/think-big">Think Big</a> energy projects and a wage and price freeze from 1982. His combative style divided the country, and he lost the snap election he called in 1984.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/robert-muldoon.jpg" alt="Robert Muldoon" width="320" height="469" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Archives New Zealand from New Zealand · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Robert_Muldoon_1978.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
